ass: wake workers for events to render, and count the fast cores

Two things about the render threads, neither of which changes what is
rendered.

A frame with two or more events woke a worker, and each worker that got an
event woke another. But an event whose image list is cached from its last
frame is handed out again in microseconds: a still sign with a line of
dialogue over it, the common frame, woke a worker 24 times a second to
find the queue empty. Workers are now woken only when two or more events
actually need rendering, and at most one thread per such event.

The default thread count was the number of cores. A frame is done when its
slowest event is, and with a thread per core that is whichever event a
little core picked up, at the frequency that core idles at. Rendering a
heavily typeset scene of 40 s (959 frames) at its frame rate inside a
foreground app (top-app cpuset), frames that took longer than a frame's
duration, by thread count:

                              1    2    4    8
  Pixel 7 (2 + 2 + 4 cores)   5    8   13   31
  Galaxy Z Fold3 (1 + 3 + 4)  1    1    0    1
  Shield TV (4 alike)         8         1

and the Pixel's CPU time for the scene went from 7.9 s with one thread to
19.4 s with eight. Where the kernel tells how fast each CPU is
(cpu_capacity, else the highest frequency), the default now counts the
CPUs at least three quarters as fast as the fastest: 2 on the Pixel, 4 on
the Fold, all of them where they are alike. ass_set_threads with a number
is unchanged.

